{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "40",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old male presented with blood in the urine and a dull, aching pain in the right side of the lower back. General examination did not reveal any abnormalities, with no swollen lymph nodes or bone tenderness. The chest, cardiovascular, and neurological examinations were normal. Abdominal examination identified a mildly tender mass in the right lumbar region. Routine laboratory tests, including a complete blood count, chest X-ray, and renal function tests, were normal. Urine analysis showed a significant number of red blood cells under high power field.\n\nAn ultrasound examination of the abdomen revealed an echogenic mass in the upper part of the right kidney measuring 11 × 10 cm. No calcified areas were noted; the renal pelvis and renal vein were not involved. The other abdominal viscera were radiologically normal. There was no lymph node enlargement. Microscopically, the tumor was highly cellular comprising of epithelial, blastemic and stromal elements. The epithelial element comprised of tubules admixed with blastemic cells. The tubular lining cells had elongated, molded, and wedge shaped nuclei exhibiting frequent mitoses. The tubular pattern resembled pseudo-rosettes at places. The blastemic component had solid areas showing cells with oval nuclei and scanty cytoplasm. The stroma with spindle cells had no specific differentiation. There was no area showing anaplasia.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
